<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kebon Kopi II inscription</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">932</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kebon Kopi II inscription is a stone inscription found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Bogor</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-malay">Old Malay</a> and generally read as dated 932 CE. It records that a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> had been restored to power, making it the earliest securely dated record naming the Sunda kingdom. It was found near the earlier Kebon Kopi I inscription of the Tarumanagara period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kebon-kopi-ii-inscription.jpg" alt="Kebon Kopi II inscription" width="320" height="530" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F. D. K. Bosch · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beschreven_steen_van_Kebon_Kopi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
